## 2. Small - a few hours, one or two files, no design decision

`sidebar-resize`  Make the chat list's width the reader's: drag the edge, keep it per browser. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE: a grip on the rail's right edge (#sidebar-resize in index.html, .sidebar-resize in app.css) drags the
    width with pointer events; arrow keys move it when the grip has focus (Shift = 40 px, Home = default); it is kept
    in localStorage as strata.sidebarw.  The width is applied as the --sidebar-w custom property
    (app.css: .chat-sidebar { width: var(--sidebar-w, 260px) }), never as style.width - an inline width would beat the
    collapsed rail's rule (.chat-sidebar[data-open="false"] { width: 60px; }) and the rail would stay open-wide.
    No grip on the collapsed rail and none on a touch screen ((hover: none)); the handler ignores a drag there too.
    Limits: 180..560 px, and never more than the window leaves (innerWidth - 320).  A stored width that is not a
    plausible number is REJECTED, not clamped, so the page keeps its 260 px (the human asked for a sanity checker:
    40, 99999, "wide", null, NaN, {} all fall back); one that is plausible but only fits a wider screen is clamped to
    the screen.  Kept OUT of DEFAULTS deliberately: it is not a drawer control and drawerDirty() must not see it
    (LESSONS.md).  Nothing is written until the reader drags, so a fresh page is the page from the last release.
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.js cases 84 and 85 (harness 75 -> 77 ok lines) and one check in
    serve/test_chat_sidebar.py (fast suite 191 -> 192).  Controls: dropping the setProperty line fails 84, trusting
    the stored number fails 85, removing the CSS var fails the python check.  Not checked: how the grip feels in a
    real browser, and touch behaviour (the grip is hidden there by design).

`sidebar-load-more`  Paginate the sidebar: render recent chats, "Show older" loads the rest. [ui]
    renderSidebar (serve/web/app.js) builds one row for EVERY chat, no cap - thousands of chats
    is thousands of DOM nodes in a 260 px rail.  Bound it: render the first N of the sorted list
    (it is already newest-first) and a "Show older" footer button that raises N by a page.
    UI only, and it works against the in-memory `chats` array, so it does NOT fix the load cost -
    that is item sidebar-capped-load.  Do it first anyway: it is the cheap half, and it is the
    affordance the capped load reuses.
    Care: keep search working.  The filter runs over `chats` on every render, so a bigger N must
    re-run it, and "no chat matches" must not appear while older chats are simply not loaded yet -
    say "load older to search further" instead.

`fork-chat`  Fork a conversation (clone, not nested). [ui]
    A chat is an object with a messages array in IndexedDB; a deep copy is short.
    Needs the same care as item chat-row-menu's Copy: a new id, and `unread`/draft state should not
    carry over.

`read-eta`  ETAs while a prompt is being read. [ui] probably, [py] maybe
    Honest assessment: the raw material is there and it is cheap to approximate, but a good
    ETA is not. `live.state === "reading"` with `prompt_read` / `prompt_total` already drives
    the percentage in the pill (app.js renderPill), and prefill rate is measured
    (`prefill_tok_s_mean`). So `(prompt_total - prompt_read) / prefill_rate` is a tooltip away.
    Why it is rough: the read rate is not constant (chunk sizes ramp, the expert cache warms,
    a parked conversation skips the read entirely), and a reused prefix is not read at all,
    so the naive number overshoots on warm chats. Measured on this box: cold reads run
    ~2,000 tok/s and a 533,954-token prompt took 332 s.
    Recommendation: do the tooltip, label it an estimate, and show it only once at least a
    few thousand tokens have been read so the rate is real. Cheap, and the pill already has
    the space. Not worth a server change.

`finish-sound`  A notification sound when a turn finishes and the window is not visible. [ui]
    The want: hear it, the way item sidebar-yesterday ("start_beep") does for the server, but for a finished
    conversation. Gate it on the same condition as the blue dot from item chat-state-marker - the turn ended
    in a chat you were not looking at, or the window was hidden.
    Nothing exists today: `grep -c "AudioContext" serve/web/app.js` is 0, and the browser
    blocks autoplay before a first gesture, so it needs the click-armed pattern item sidebar-yesterday hit
    head-on (the rejected client-side attempt for start_beep is in LESSONS.md). A short
    WebAudio oscillator is a few lines; a file asset is not needed.
    Two decisions before building: a sound for "finished while hidden" only, or also for
    "finished in another chat while this window is visible"? And should it be one setting or
    follow the blue dot exactly? Recommend: follow the dot, one setting, off by default.
    Do NOT use the Notification API for the first version - it needs a permission prompt and
    behaves differently on Windows/TDE. Sound first, badge later if wanted.

`unread-in-open-chat`  A blue dot when a reply finishes in the chat you are on but you are not near the bottom. [ui]
    Re-scoped 2026-10-24 with the human and parked to do poll-on-enter first.  This is the SAME sidebar dot as the
    per-chat `c.unread` in the row's left gutter (serve/web/app.js, chatItem: `state = ... c.unread ? "new" : ""`) -
    NOT a new per-message indicator (the old wording "the gutter slot item chat-state-marker added" meant the
    sidebar gutter, which already exists; there is no per-message gutter).  Today it is set only when the turn's
    chat is not the open one - the `turn.chatId !== chatId` guard in send().
    Wanted: also set it when you ARE in that chat but were not near the bottom when the turn ended, and clear it
    when you scroll back to the bottom.
    How, reusing what exists:
      - Trigger on turn END, not on scrolling.  That is what satisfies "do not mark unread just because the reader
        scrolled up deliberately" - the dot appears because a reply finished, never from scroll position alone.
      - The measurement is the follow decision from item auto-scroll-big-block: send()'s final paint already
        computes `const follow = nearBottom()`.  Set c.unread when `!follow` and the turn delivered output (the
        hasOutput predicate).  Reorder send() so the unread decision runs after that follow is known.
      - Clearing needs a NEW scroll listener on #chat-scroll (there is none today - only click/toggle on #chat):
        when nearBottom() becomes true, clear the open chat's unread and re-render the sidebar.  renderChat forces
        the scroll to the bottom (scrollDown(true)), so switching into a chat clears it, as showChat already does.
    It is a moderate change (a scroll listener + reordering the turn-end logic), not a one-liner - which is why it
    was parked for poll-on-enter.

`chat-row-menu`  A three-dot menu on each chat row: Rename, Copy, Export, Delete. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Replaces the two hover buttons (`.chat-item__actions`, app.css) with one overflow button
    that opens a menu.
    Cheap parts: Rename and Delete already exist (renameChat, deleteChat) and just move into
    the menu. Copy means DUPLICATE the conversation, not copy-to-clipboard: it creates a second chat
    with the same messages and a new id, sitting beside the first in the list (not nested). So it is
    the same operation as item fork-chat - build one clone function (new id, fresh time, and do not
    carry over `unread`/draft state) and wire it to both this menu item and fork-chat. Export is the
    separate file/Markdown path (item import-export), not the clipboard.
    The cost is the menu itself: a popover positioned in a 260 px rail that is `overflow-y:
    auto`, so it can be clipped by the sidebar's scroll container. That needs either a
    fixed-position popover or flipping the row's direction near the bottom. Budget more time
    for that than for the actions.
    Watch: the actions already only appear on hover and reserve no width (they are
    `display: none` until hovered, and the state markers live in the left gutter) - keep that
    property, or the titles shrink again.

`system-prompt`  Add and edit a system prompt, set ONCE when the conversation starts. [ui] [py] EASY
    There is no system-prompt field in the web app today (no system_prompt anywhere in
    app.js), and the server already supports one: a leading system/developer message is
    merged into the template's system turn (chat_template.jinja). So this is a per-chat
    field stored with the chat object in IndexedDB, sent as the first message on every
    request of that chat. Because it never changes mid-chat, it costs nothing: the system
    turn stays identical turn to turn and the pinned root checkpoint keeps matching.
    Editing it LATER is the only case that re-reads the chat once - worth a confirm dialog,
    same as the tool-thinking toggle.
    This is the prerequisite for item warm-then-cancel (dynamic system prompt) and it makes item pin-conversation's
    prefix pin actually useful, so it is worth doing early despite being small.


`store-full-attachments`  Keep the original bytes of attached files/pictures in IndexedDB. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    cleanMessages now keeps the image data URL and file text in the IndexedDB record (keepBytes defaults true), so
    a reloaded post can still show and open its attachments - the "not kept in the browser" note is gone for
    history-on chats.  The localStorage single-chat path (history off) still strips to names, because localStorage
    is capped around 5 MB and a data URL there could blow it.
    Still open, if records get large: cap by size, or store bytes keyed by id and fetch on demand (the trim-tool-
    results pattern), rather than inlining every data URL in the record.  Not needed until someone hits a limit.


`chat-size-stats`  Show a conversation's size, and sort the sidebar by size or name. [ui]
    Hover a sidebar row -> a popup with the name and its size, e.g. "Chat name (10.4 KB)".  And a way to sort the
    list by size and a-z, not only newest-first (renderSidebar sorts by time today).
    Size: measure the serialized record (JSON.stringify(chat).length, or the stored bytes).  Sorting by size needs
    it computed per chat; sorting a-z is chatTitle.  Related to sidebar-load-more / sidebar-capped-load (how many
    rows are even rendered).


`cull-old-chats`  Free space: delete chats older than a time, or the largest. [ui]
    The human's framing: cull old chats after a certain age, or let the user delete large chats to free space.
    Needs chat-size-stats (to rank by size) and a retention affordance (a "older than N days" / "largest first"
    view with a delete).  Keep it a deliberate user action with a confirm, not automatic deletion - deleting a
    conversation is destructive and there is no undo across a reload.  Related to item import-export (export before
    culling) and pin-conversation (which chats are worth keeping).


`edit-history`  Show that a post was edited, and maybe a history of its edits. [ui]
    Raised with item edit-posts.  Minimum: when a post is edited, stamp it and show it in the meta line, e.g.
    "You · 07:04 AM (edited)".  Store an `edited: true` (or an editedAt time) on the message when it is saved -
    that is the cheap half, and answer the human's question "does it at least update the time?": today it does
    not, the meta keeps the original `time`.
    A full edit history (prior versions, restore) is much more: it means keeping old text per message, which
    bloats the record.  Keep it a separate, later decision; do not fold it into edit-posts.


`configurable-send-keys`  Make Enter/Shift+Enter/Ctrl+Enter in the composer and edit box a setting. [ui]
    Today the composer: Enter submits, Shift+Enter is a newline (send() keydown handler).  The edit box (item
    edit-posts) used Ctrl+Enter to save, Escape to cancel - inconsistent.  The human wants them consolidated
    (probably match the composer), and made configurable: some people want Enter = newline and Shift/Ctrl+Enter =
    submit.  Add a setting (DEFAULTS + the store, like other settings) for the send-key scheme, applied to both
    boxes.  Also wanted: Enter/Ctrl+Enter in the edit box opens a small modal asking Save / Submit / Cancel, so a
    stray Enter does not fire off a resend.  Decide the default so the common case stays one keystroke.


`attach-unknown-ext`  An unknown file extension should ask what to do, not just reject. [ui]
    addFiles (serve/web/app.js) rejects anything that is not an image or a known text extension (isTextFile /
    TEXT_EXT).  Instead, when the extension is unknown, ask the user (a small prompt): attach as text, or cancel.
    Keep the binary guard (a NUL byte means binary) so a real binary still refuses with a clear reason.


`attach-pdfs`  Handle PDF attachments - as text (default) or as images. [ui] [py]
    A PDF is neither an image/ nor a text file today, so it is rejected.  Options: extract text, or render pages
    as images the model can see.  The human wants a toggle (in the side/Settings menu) to pick which way a PDF is
    handled, default text.  Text extraction needs a PDF reader; page-as-image needs rendering + the vision path,
    and the model must support images.  Decide where the work lives (front end vs server) before building.


`thinking-markdown`  Render thinking as markdown, controlled by the message's single md toggle. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Thinking bodies were plain textContent (no markdown).  Now a SETTLED thinking block renders as markdown
    (escaped first, safe) via renderThinkBody; while it is the live streaming run it stays plain text so the
    growing block is not re-parsed every frame and does not flicker.  Thinking follows the message's ONE markdown
    setting (msgMd - the global mdtheirs toggle and the per-message md button) - there is deliberately NO separate
    per-thinking-block md button (the human asked for one control, not several).  The body carries a .md class so
    markdown drops pre-wrap spacing.


