`keep-boxes-open-on-interact`  If I have interacted with a thinking/tool box (scrolled or clicked it), keep it open. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE: a scroll inside a block now counts the way a click did.  A capture-phase scroll listener on #chat (scroll
    does not bubble) marks a thinking block dataset.touched and a tool block t.open = true, so the fold control
    leaves it alone for the rest of the turn - same lifetime as a click: dropped by send() at the turn's end, never
    written to the chat record.  Whose scroll is it?  The page follows its own output by moving a body to its end,
    which leaves the body near its end, so a body that ends up near its end (nearEnd(box, 48) - the same slack the
    follow rule uses) is NOT read as the reader's.  A comparison against intent, not a consumed flag (LESSONS.md:
    a browser queues scroll events the way it queues toggle events).
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.js case 87 runs one stream three ways - no scroll, the page's own follow, and
    the reader scrolling away from the end - and asserts the fold happens in the first two and not the third.
    Controls: marking nothing fails the reader run; dropping the near-end guard fails the follow run.
    Unverified in a browser: nothing marks a block from a real wheel/touch scroll yet, since the stub fires no
    scroll event when script sets scrollTop - the near-end comparison is reasoned, not measured.

`tool-fold-on-next-output`  A finished tool call should stay open until the next output starts, not fold the instant its result lands. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
