`autoscroll-cooldown`  Auto-scroll should back off when the reader fights it. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE: the follow rule (nearBottom(), 120 px of slack) is now gated on a hold.  A scroll listener on #chat-scroll
    compares the chat's position against autoTop - where stickBottom LEFT it, read back after the assignment,
    because a real browser clamps scrollTop to the bottom - and a position more than 24 px above that is the
    reader fighting, so follow is held off for 2500 ms.  Coming back to the bottom clears it immediately; a forced
    scroll (renderChat, a chat switch) clears it too.  paint() and the final paint both use following().
    A comparison against intent, not a consumed flag: the page's own scroll fires the same event (LESSONS.md).
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.js case 89 drives send() with a growing answer - stick, stick, reader up 100 px
    (inside the 120 px slack), frame does not drag, reader back down, follow resumes.  Control: following() without
    the hold fails the "does not drag them back" assertion.
    NOT measured in a browser: the stub never clamps scrollTop, so the read-back (autoTop = s.scrollTop, not
    scrollHeight) is reasoned from how a real browser behaves - if the follow ever switches itself off for good,
    that line is the first suspect.

`test-suite-speed`  The suites spend almost all their time asleep, not working. [py] [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    Measured 2026-11-19 on this box: the fast suite was 194 tests in 43.6 s, but the node harness was 7.97 s wall
    against 0.30 s CPU (3%), and every test in serve/test_static_cache cost a uniform ~520 ms.  Two causes:
      - serve/test_chat_sidebar.js awaited setTimeout(r, 30) 228 times = 6.8 s of sleep.  It now yields with
        setImmediate, which still lands after the microtasks the stub IndexedDB and stub fetch answer on: the
        harness is 7.97 s -> 0.19 s.
      - serve() ran httpd.serve_forever() with its default poll_interval of 0.5 s, so every shutdown() in every
        test waited out a poll.  serve() now passes poll_interval 0.02 (serve/server.py, serve()).  Fast suite
        36.8 s -> 9.4 s; serve.test_server 94.6 s -> 54.7 s (317 tests, both OK).  For the running server the
        same line means a restart notices in <=20 ms instead of <=500 ms, and 50 idle wakeups a second instead
        of 2 - negligible against one request, but it is a production default, so it needs the human's restart.
    Still open: the remaining time is real work (scripted engine delays, watchdog and lifecycle tests), and
    67 serve(port=0) call sites across 27 test files mean the modules could run in parallel - that is item
    test-command's shape to decide (sh vs py vs bat), not a speed fix.

`drop-single-chat`  Kill the "Chat history" switch: the list is always the page. [ui] DONE 2026-11-19
    DONE (the human's call: "no one should ever use it"): the About switch, the `historyOn` flag and every
    off-branch are gone - the one-chat-in-localStorage mode, the New-chat-clears-it-with-an-Undo path, and the
    `keepBytes=false` argument that only that path used (a switch nothing can set is a switch that gets misread
    later).  What SURVIVES is the migration: openHistory still reads an old "strata.chat" key and turns it into
    the first record, so a browser that only ever had the single chat does not open on an empty list.
    A browser that refuses IndexedDB (private mode) keeps working with the list in memory and still says so
    (openHistory's warnStorage).
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.py rewrote six checks that pinned the off mode (the page markup, "opt-in and
    off is the last release" -> "the mode is gone and its key is migrated", "the store is only used when the
    switch is on" -> "opened at start, memory-only still works", "turning the switch off keeps the chat on screen"
    -> "New chat never clears the chat you are in", the attachment check, the draft check).  Harness case 1
    rewritten the same way, case 11 (flip the switch off) deleted; 85 -> 84 ok lines.
    Controls: putting `hidden` back on #sidebar fails the page check; writing to "strata.chat" again fails the
    migration check; and the removal caught one real bug on its own - start-up called openHistory(false) without
    the .then(renderChat) that setHistory used to do, so the list was read and never drawn (harness: "the list
    shows them - 0 !== 4").

## 3. Medium - two or three layers, or a decision to make first (days)

`trim-tool-results`  Trim long tool calls before they reach the DOM. [py] [ui] - decide the policy first
    The question "do we trim?" has a measured answer for this box: `max_result_chars` is
    **500,000** in strata-iq3_s.json (the code default is 20,000), and `max_rounds` is 900.
    So one call can put 500 KB of text into the page, and a long agent turn can do that many
    times. The DOM cap is only visual: `.tool-call__pre` has `max-height: 320px; overflow:
    auto` (app.css:113), which bounds the *box*, not the text node inside it.
    So yes, this can overwhelm the page, and it is partly mitigated already: an open block's
    body is built lazily and only while open (syncToolBody), so a closed call costs nothing.
    The exposure is (a) a call the reader opens, and (b) every result kept in the message
    object and written to IndexedDB.
    Options, in order of preference:
      - cap what goes into the DOM per open block (say 100 KB) with an explicit
        "showing the first 100 KB of 500 KB - Download / Copy full" affordance. Keeps the
        full text for copy and export, bounds the render.
      - keep only a preview in the message object and store the full result once, keyed by
        call id, fetched on demand. Bigger change, but it is the one that fixes IndexedDB
        growth too.
      - lower `max_result_chars` in the config. Cheapest, but it changes what the MODEL
        sees, which is a different decision from what the browser renders - do not conflate
        them.
    Measure before building: how big is a real chat record here, and how many open blocks
    does a 900-round turn actually produce?

`import-export`  Import and export conversations. [ui] [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    DONE: a Settings gear in the sidebar opens a modal with Export / Import / Delete conversations.  All three use
    one chat-picker: a checkbox per chat (default on), a name filter, a date range (from-only = everything on/after;
    to-only = everything on/before; both = the range), and All/None that act on the VISIBLE rows - but the action
    applies to every CHECKED chat even if the current filter hides it.  Format is llama.cpp-compatible JSONL (one
    chat per line, OpenAI-shaped `messages`) PLUS a `strata` block carrying ids, time, reasoning, tool rounds with
    at/rat, files and usage, so thinking + tool rounds round-trip (a pure llama.cpp file would lose them and break
    the replay - the note below).  Import parses both (with or without the extras).  The sidebar now shows each
    chat's size and attachment count.
    DONE (also): a global "Enable attachments" toggle (default on) that gates the composer's attach button and
    addFiles - surfaced inside the Export and Import pickers.  The Delete picker has a "Delete only attachments
    (keep the chats)" toggle at the bottom that routes the action to strip attachments vs delete the chats.  The
    picker's bottom button is "Back" (returns to the Chat settings menu); Escape and backdrop-click still close.
    Size/attachment count shows in the picker rows (not the sidebar list).
    Note: tool rounds carry `at` and `rat` offsets that the replay depends on (assistantMessages, and
    serve/server.py on why they must match). An import that loses them re-creates the re-read bug - hence the
    `strata` block round-trips them; a strictly-llama.cpp import can't preserve them.
    FIXED (import of real llama.cpp exports): the importer now handles all three shapes - the NEW export (a .zip of
    per-conversation .jsonl: a session header + a message TREE with parent/children + currNode, toolCalls and
    toolCallId, reasoningContent), the OLD export (a .json array of {conv, messages}), and our own .jsonl.  It walks
    the active branch (currNode -> parents, reversed), drops system/root messages, groups assistant+tool runs into
    Strata turns with at/rat, and restores thinking from reasoningContent.  Zip is read with the browser's
    DecompressionStream (deflate-raw).  Verified against the real 2026-10-26 export (a 282-message tool-heavy
    branch groups with every tool result matched).
    FIXED (attachment chats not listed): the picker called chatSize -> JSON.stringify on every chat, and 800+ huge
    chats froze the list.  chatSize is now a cheap sum of string lengths.  Also mapped llama.cpp's `extra`
    attachments (TEXT -> file, IMAGE -> image with base64Url) so imported chats keep their attachments.  Added a
    "Zip (one .jsonl per chat)" toggle to Export (writes a STORE-method zip via crc32, round-trips through the
    importer).  Raised the toast layer above the modals so import warnings are readable.

`edit-posts`  Edit and delete posts in a conversation, mine and the model's. [ui] [py] PARTLY DONE 2026-10-24
    PROGRESS (built in increments, decided with the human 2026-10-24):
      - DONE (increment 1): message ids (mid(), backfilled on load, identity only - never sent, so the replay is
        untouched); an Edit button on each post (openEditBox, an inline textarea); Save edits the text in place and
        keeps every message below it (the "edit only, do not resubmit" path); Cancel/Escape confirm only when the
        text changed; a turn that made tool calls is LOCKED (canEditMsg) because its rounds are offsets into its own
        text (at/rat) and editing that breaks the replay.
      - DONE (increment 2): Submit on a user post - send(editOf) saves the edit, drops every message after it, and
        resends a fresh turn; the box shows Save / Submit / Cancel for user posts and Save / Cancel for assistant
        posts.  (The inline re-read warning is not shown yet.)
      - DONE (increment 3): the tool-call edit modal.  Editing a turn that made calls opens a mode menu (a fixed
        popover, like the row menu) with "Edit final output only" (keeps the tool rounds and their at/rat offsets,
        edits only the text after the last call), "Edit entire (drops its tool calls)" (replaces the text, clears
        m.tools), or Cancel.  The Edit button is now offered on every post.
      - TODO: the inline re-read warning on Submit; keyboard consolidation (see configurable-send-keys).
      - DONE (delete-a-post): a Delete button on every post opens a modal (not a bare confirm) with a "delete all
        messages below" checkbox - default ON (truncate at that message, the safe case), or OFF to remove just that
        one (leaves the turns after it).  It warns the action is undoable only for the session and will re-read the
        conversation from there (~N tokens).  Blocked while a turn runs, and it discards an open edit first.
      - FIXED (streaming + submit polish): tool calls count in the live meta as soon as they start (not only after
        they finish); the meta row stays visible during a turn so stats show while thinking, not only once output
        arrives; the new turn's row is marked .streaming immediately so the Edit button never flickers; and Submit
        now opens a resend-confirm modal (confirmResend) like delete - it drops everything after the edited post
        and re-reads, so it should not fire on a stray click.
      - FIXED (context accuracy): the poll's live prompt/generated figures are `null` between MCP rounds (shown=
        false when idle), so the message's context collapsed to ~0 in those gaps.  poll() now HOLDS the last
        non-null figures across the gaps (lastLive merge), send() resets them per turn, and liveMeta uses the
        exact `generated` (not a character estimate) so the count no longer jumps at the end.  A stopped turn now
        keeps its token count (from the held live figures) instead of just "Stopped".  The final context is exact
        (from the usage chunk) so it drops the "~"; only the live estimate keeps the "~".
      - FIXED (the "back to 0 tokens" bug): the poll's `generated` counts the CURRENT round only and resets to 0
        when a new round starts after a tool call, so the live count dropped each round.  noteLive() now
        accumulates it across rounds (m._gen, resetting the round baseline when `generated` falls), so the token
        count climbs monotonically for the whole turn.
      - DONE (attachments in the edit box): editing a user post lists its images/files as removable chips plus an
        Attach button (a paperclip, like the composer's; reuses addFiles/renderAttachments with a per-box list).
        Removing and saving updates the post.  Caveat: a reloaded post's attachments are stored name-only, so they
        show as removable chips but the original bytes cannot be re-attached - item store-full-attachments.  Adding
        via the picker is browser-only (FileReader), not unit-tested.
      - FIXED (attachment bugs from the human): saving an edit rebuilds the whole ROW (not just the bubble), so
        thumbnails/chips update without a reload; the Attach button is the paperclip icon; image chips carry a
        thumbnail; the message's own read-only attachment display is hidden while editing (attachments show once,
        as the editable pills with trash icons); clickable chips get a hand cursor; message thumbnails keep their
        aspect ratio and are large.  Clicking a message IMAGE opens it in a new tab via a blob: URL (data: URLs are
        blocked for top-level navigation - the "Not allowed to navigate top frame to data URL" error); clicking a
        text-file chip opens a viewer with its contents; Escape/backdrop close the viewer.  Attachment bytes are now
        kept in the IndexedDB record, so reload no longer loses them (see store-full-attachments).
      - DONE (live meta + guards): a streaming message's meta updates about once a second with an ESTIMATE of
        tokens/rate/tool-calls/context (liveMeta in serve/web/app.js - token count from characters, ~4/word),
        replaced by the exact usage figures when the turn ends; the Edit button is hidden while streaming (the row
        gets a .streaming class, matching the copy button which was already hidden); and deleting a chat now asks
        to confirm first (Undo is still offered for the session).
      - FIXED (bug reports from the human): "Edit entire" left the tool blocks on screen until a reload - the
        redraw now rebuilds the whole bubble, not incrementally; only one edit box at a time (clicking Edit on
        another post asks to discard, a second click on the same post is a no-op, and the editing row hides its
        own Edit button); clicking Edit during a live turn now says "Stop the answer first" instead of doing
        nothing; the edit box scrolls its buttons into view.
      - DONE (option B): editing a turn that has thinking shows a "Delete the model's thinking" checkbox - kept by
        default, checked by default for "Edit entire" (which already drops the tool calls); a user post has none.
        Editing the thinking text itself (option C) stays out: the per-round rat offsets are replay-critical.
      - Assistant posts: Save only (no Submit, no attach).  User posts: Save + Submit + attach.
    Chat-level rename and delete exist (renameChat, deleteChat); message-level delete does not yet.
    Straightforward to build. See item edit-no-reread for the cost.
    The delete wants a modal, not a confirm(): "delete this message", a warning that it
    cannot be undone and that the conversation must be re-read (until item edit-no-reread lands), and a
    checkbox, **checked by default**, "delete all messages below". Unchecked means delete
    only that one and leave the rest - which is the case that changes the prompt mid-stream
    and therefore the one that re-reads everything after it. The modal should say which of
    the two it is about to do, in token terms if the numbers are available (item context-percent already
    computes them).
    Message-level identity is a prerequisite for any of this being safe: messages have no ids
    today, only array position and `time`. See item nvme-park.

`edit-persist-across-chats`  Let an open edit survive switching chats (and a reload). [ui]
    Today switching chats while editing prompts to discard (confirmDiscardEdit in serve/web/app.js) so the edit is
    not silently lost.  The nicer behaviour: keep the edit box open per chat, so you can switch away and back and
    the in-progress edit is still there.  That means storing the draft edit (text + attachments + thinking choice)
    keyed by chat id - in memory for switching, and in the browser (localStorage/IndexedDB) so a reload restores
    it, like the composer draft already does per chat.  Decide whether one edit per chat is enough or several.


`queue-another-chat`  Queue another conversation while one is going (llama.cpp style). [ui] MEDIUM, not engine work
    The SERVER ALREADY DOES THIS: docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request
    at a time: the others wait in the server's queue." Nothing engine-side is missing, and
    --batch N would run them at once instead of queued.
    What is missing is entirely in the page: one busy flag, one send button disabled
    (app.js:1015), one stream reader. Needs per-request state (controller, target message,
    chat id) so two streams can be tracked and painted into the right chat.

`offline-outbox`  Queue a request when the server is down and send it when it is back. [ui] MEDIUM
    Today the page just errors. The SERVER side is already right: requests queue behind the
    FIFO (docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request at a time: the others
    wait in the server's queue"), and a restart never hangs a waiter - it ends with a clean
    503 (serve/test_restart_waiters.py, #1012). So this is entirely client-side.
    Needs: an outbox in IndexedDB (the store already exists), a health poll, ordering rules,
    and a decision about a second message arriving while one is queued. It must survive a
    reload, which is what makes it an IndexedDB problem rather than an array.

