    this panel) picks the side with more room, caps the height to it, and the panel scrolls inside that.
    THE MONITOR CARD NOW MIRRORS THE CHAT ON SCREEN (the human's ask, and the answer to the question I asked):
    its chips read the chat's list, so the wrench menu and the card never disagree, and nothing else is touched.
    What moves the defaults is a separate "Use these tools as the defaults" button on the card, shown only when
    the chat differs; it sends one {"server": ..., "tools": [names that should be ON]} change per server, and
    runconfig.set_mcp turns that into disabled_tools = (what the hub lists) - (what should be on).  The hub has to
    be asked for the names: the config never lists them, and guessing an empty list would have switched every
    tool of that server off, so an unknown server is a 400 instead.
    ONE LOOK, NOT TWO (the human asked twice): the panel is built from the Monitor card's own markup -
    .mcp-server / .mcp-server__head (the st-toggle switch, the name, "N of M tools") / .mcp-server__tools with the
    chips - so there is a single style for "which tools" and no second one to keep in step.  The picker's own
    .mcp-picker__server / .mcp-server-switch / .mcp-picker__tools rules are deleted; only two spacing overrides
    remain inside .mcp-picker.
    THEN IT STILL LOOKED WRONG, and the cause was the cascade, not the markup: the row menu styles its items with
    a bare descendant rule on the popover, and the picker IS a popover (.chat-menu), so that rule reached every
    chip and every switch inside it and turned them into full-width block rows.  The items now carry
    .chat-menu__item and the three rules are scoped to it.  The lesson is the one LESSONS.md already carries about
    reuse: sharing a container class shares its CSS, so a "generic popover" class is not free.
    NO REFLOW, AND RESET KEEPS THE PANEL OPEN (the human: the Reset button "causes everything to jump").  Showing
    the cost line and Reset only when they apply reads fine in a screenshot and badly in use - the panel resized
    on every click.  They now live in one footer row that is always there at the same height: the cost text is
    empty until something changes, and Reset is greyed (disabled, with a title saying why) when the chat already
    matches the defaults.  The Monitor card's Apply gets the same treatment, so the card does not reflow either.
    Reset rebuilds the panel in place instead of closing it - the panel is where the work is happening.
    THE WARNING IS STATE, NOT "DID YOU CLICK" (the human: it "should disappear when it changes back").  send()
    records what the chat actually sent (c.mcpSent), and the cost text shows while the chat's list differs from
    THAT.  So ticking something back clears it, and it survives closing and reopening the panel until you send -
    which is the honest version: the re-read is coming whether or not the panel is open.  The session flag
    (`changed`, and a data-changed attribute on the panel) is gone with it.
