    cached prefix.

`poll-on-enter`  Pressing Enter does not poll, so the pill can read Idle for 15 s into a turn. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Measured: neither send() nor setBusy() calls pollNow(). The chain is armed by the previous poll's
    pollDelay (app.js:223 and 232), and with nothing running pollDelay returns POLL_IDLE_MS = 15000
    (app.js:164, 177). So after Enter the next /metrics is whatever was already scheduled, up to 15 s
    away, and the pill still says Idle while the engine reads the prompt. showTab already solves exactly
    this for a tab change (app.js:87). Fix: setBusy(true) calls pollNow(); one scheduler already means it
    cannot fork a second chain (item metrics-lite's lesson). Test: drive setBusy(true) in the harness and assert the
    recorded setTimeout delay is 0, and that a second send does not start a second chain.

`recover-noop`  "recover" was a silent no-op for a request that asks low or medium effort. [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    focused_recovery_prompt (serve/server.py:193) looks only for the xhigh sentence. A request that asked low
    or medium carries that level's sentence instead, so the token subsequence is absent, the function returns
    None, and the loop is detected with "recover" configured while nothing is recovered. Measured in
    strata-slow-death.txt: a 63-token prompt at effort_position "start" has no xhigh sentence and returns
    None; at "end" the sentence is present and it returns 93 ids.
    Who was bitten: not this box's own web traffic (the app sends high, mapped to xhigh at
    chat_template.jinja:59-61), but any other client that asks low or medium - common as soon as a config or
    an app sets reasoning_effort low. This config sets no effort_position, so it is the default "start".
    FIXED: the splice now keys on the sentence actually in the prompt rather than on the level it assumes.
    server.py:193 - xhigh or medium sentence present -> replaced by the low one; no sentence at all (what the
    template does for medium, chat_template.jinja:66-70) -> the low one inserted at the head of the system
    turn, anchored on the role's ids and None rather than a blind splice if they are not a run; already low ->
    None, because there is nothing lower to ask for. The caller no longer does that in silence: server.py:4094
    says once that the recovery cannot lower this request, and it does not print the "resuming the same output"
    line for work it did not do.
    HOW TO CHECK IT WITHOUT AN ENGINE OR A GPU (the recipe that made the diagnosis credible, so the log file
    itself is not needed): render one small request with the server's own functions at each effort level, then
    call focused_recovery_prompt on the ids. At effort_position "start" with low or medium the xhigh sentence
    is absent and it returns None; at "end" the sentence is present and it returns a spliced prompt.
    strata-slow-death.txt is deliberately NOT committed: it is pasted live model output and carries the
    template's boundary tags as literal text, which is what cut two turns today. Everything worth keeping
    from it is in this item and in slow-death.

    Tests: serve/test_reasoning_loop_recovery.py 18 -> 22. Two controls, each failing on the assertion meant to
    catch it: swapping the original xhigh-only function back in fails the medium-insert test, and putting the
    silent caller back fails the log test. A first control (dropping "medium" from the levels tuple) PASSED,
    which is what showed the insert path already covered that case and the control was testing nothing.
    STILL OPEN: what to do when the request is already at low. It now logs and lets the thinking run to the
    budget, as before; ending it there instead (the "stop" behaviour) is the alternative and is not chosen.

`switch-while-running`  Read and switch to another conversation while one is running. [ui] DONE 2026-10-23
    Shipped in 85084ba6. It was NOT the three-line change this entry once claimed - the entry was corrected
    first, and the correction was right: showChat calls renderChat(), which removes every .st-msg, while send()
    had captured one element ($("chat").lastElementChild) and painted into it for the whole turn, so simply
    dropping the guard would have made the answer vanish mid-stream, silently.
    What it took instead: the turn carries the conversation it belongs to (busy = {controller, msg, chat,
    chatId}); rowOf(m) resolves the row by the message's index in the chat on screen, every frame (no row means
    another chat is open - the turn keeps running, it just does not paint); and saveChat(c) writes the turn's own
    chat rather than whichever one happens to be open.
    Guards now: openChat and startChat are free (switching is the point); deleteChat still refuses for the chat
    that OWNS a live turn (c.id === busy.chatId); and with the sidebar off, "New chat" still refuses, because
    there it CLEARS the one chat instead of switching.
    Unlocked by this: item queue-another-chat (queue another conversation) and item chat-state-marker (mark a chat whose reply finished while
    you were not looking at it) - both were impossible while switching was refused.
`tool-args-stream`  Show what the model is typing inside a tool call instead of "(being written)". [py] [ui] DONE 2026-10-23
    Shipped in 43fb9845. The half-built plumbing the entry described was exactly right: the parser already
    emits tool_start and tool_args pieces, and the MCP loop threw the pieces away with a bare `continue`
    ("its argument pieces are not streamed to the client").
    Server: run_with_mcp takes stream_args and, only then, yields {"event": "args", "id", "text"} per piece.
    The flag comes from the request (Service.mcp_stream_args, mirroring mcp_keep_thinking), so a client that
    does not ask gets byte-identical chunks - test_mcp's existing test still asserts start/call/result
    exactly. The pieces never enter the OpenAI-style deltas either, so nothing is duplicated.
    Page: sends strata_mcp_args, accumulates the pieces on the tool (t.argsText), and shows them in the
    arguments pre and in the collapsed summary line as they arrive - so you can watch a big call being
    written without opening it. The args box follows the bottom the same way the thinking does, only if the
    reader was already there. When the finished call lands, arguments wins and argsText is dropped, so the
    record does not carry both.
    test_mcp asserts the pieces reassemble into the finished arguments and belong to that call.
